1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,008
Welcome to Unit Four of Algebra One:
functions.

2
00:00:03,458 --> 00:00:08,228
A function is one of the most important
ideas in all of math. In this lesson,

3
00:00:08,228 --> 00:00:12,503
we'll learn what a relation is, and what
makes a relation a function.

4
00:00:13,633 --> 00:00:18,895
A relation is any set of input-output
pairs. For example: one goes to four, two

5
00:00:18,895 --> 00:00:21,292
goes to five, and three goes to six.

6
00:00:21,742 --> 00:00:26,842
We can write these as ordered pairs: one
comma four, two comma five, three comma

7
00:00:26,842 --> 00:00:27,097
six.

8
00:00:28,233 --> 00:00:32,436
A function is a relation where every input
has exactly one output.

9
00:00:32,886 --> 00:00:35,019
Each input gets one answer, never two.

10
00:00:36,167 --> 00:00:41,571
Look at this mapping diagram. One goes to
three, two goes to five, and three also

11
00:00:41,571 --> 00:00:42,439
goes to five.

12
00:00:42,889 --> 00:00:47,949
Two inputs share the output five, and
that's fine. Every input still has exactly

13
00:00:47,949 --> 00:00:50,099
one output, so this is a function.

14
00:00:51,233 --> 00:00:55,308
Now look at this one. One goes to two, and
one also goes to seven.

15
00:00:55,758 --> 00:01:00,195
The input one has two different outputs,
so this relation is not a function.

16
00:01:01,333 --> 00:01:06,443
On a graph, use the vertical line test. If
any vertical line crosses the graph more

17
00:01:06,443 --> 00:01:08,352
than once, it's not a function.

18
00:01:08,802 --> 00:01:14,321
A line or a parabola passes the test. A
sideways parabola fails, because one

19
00:01:14,321 --> 00:01:16,354
vertical line hits it twice.

20
00:01:17,500 --> 00:01:22,494
Your turn. Is this set of pairs a
function? Two comma one, four comma one,

21
00:01:22,494 --> 00:01:24,924
six comma three, and two comma five.

22
00:01:25,374 --> 00:01:26,825
Pause the video and try it.

23
00:01:27,275 --> 00:01:32,075
The input two appears with two outputs,
one and five. So it is not a function.

24
00:01:33,200 --> 00:01:37,061
Let's check three relations to decide
whether each one is a function.

25
00:01:38,500 --> 00:01:43,802
Decide which of these relations are
functions. Relation A is the set of pairs

26
00:01:43,802 --> 00:01:49,587
two five, three seven, two nine, and four
one. Relation B is negative one four, zero

27
00:01:49,587 --> 00:01:54,820
four, one four, and two four. Relation C
is the graph of x equals y squared.

28
00:01:55,967 --> 00:02:01,636
In relation A, the input two goes to both
five and nine. One input with two outputs

29
00:02:01,636 --> 00:02:02,729
breaks the rule.

30
00:02:03,867 --> 00:02:05,232
So A is not a function.

31
00:02:06,367 --> 00:02:11,592
In relation B, every input appears exactly
once. All the outputs are four, but

32
00:02:11,592 --> 00:02:13,535
repeated outputs are allowed.

33
00:02:14,667 --> 00:02:17,803
So B is a function. In fact it's a
constant function.

34
00:02:18,933 --> 00:02:24,765
For C, try x equals four. y could be two
or negative two. A vertical line at x

35
00:02:24,765 --> 00:02:27,232
equals four hits the graph twice.

36
00:02:28,367 --> 00:02:31,908
So C fails the vertical line test and is
not a function.

37
00:02:33,033 --> 00:02:34,868
Only relation B is a function.

38
00:02:36,333 --> 00:02:41,570
Watch out. A repeated input with different
outputs breaks a function, but repeated

39
00:02:41,570 --> 00:02:45,976
outputs are fine. For graphs, any vertical
line can hit at most once.

40
00:02:47,100 --> 00:02:52,074
Your turn. Is the set one three, two
three, three one a function? What about

41
00:02:52,074 --> 00:02:55,804
five zero, five one, six two? Pause the
video and try it.

42
00:03:00,333 --> 00:03:05,674
The first is a function, since each input
appears once. The second is not, because

43
00:03:05,674 --> 00:03:07,693
five goes to both zero and one.

44
00:03:08,833 --> 00:03:13,807
In this lesson, we'll learn function
notation: the way mathematicians name and

45
00:03:13,807 --> 00:03:14,700
use functions.

46
00:03:15,833 --> 00:03:21,932
f of x equals two x plus three. The letter
f is the function's name, x is the input,

47
00:03:21,932 --> 00:03:23,748
and f of x is the output.

48
00:03:24,198 --> 00:03:26,928
Careful: f of x does not mean f times x.

49
00:03:28,066 --> 00:03:30,882
To evaluate f of four, replace x with
four.

50
00:03:31,332 --> 00:03:35,919
Two times four, plus three, is eleven. So
f of four equals eleven.

51
00:03:37,066 --> 00:03:38,986
Negative inputs work the same way.

52
00:03:39,436 --> 00:03:44,706
f of negative two is two times negative
two, plus three, which is negative one.

53
00:03:45,833 --> 00:03:48,670
Now let g of x equal x squared minus one.

54
00:03:49,120 --> 00:03:54,418
g of three is nine minus one, which is
eight. g of negative three is also nine

55
00:03:54,418 --> 00:03:59,104
minus one, which is eight. Different
inputs can give the same output.

56
00:04:00,233 --> 00:04:05,235
We can also work backward. If f of x
equals fifteen, then two x plus three

57
00:04:05,235 --> 00:04:06,249
equals fifteen.

58
00:04:06,699 --> 00:04:10,326
Subtract three: two x equals twelve. So x
equals six.

59
00:04:11,466 --> 00:04:17,787
Your turn. If h of x equals five minus x,
find h of seven. Then find x when h of x

60
00:04:17,787 --> 00:04:18,634
equals two.

61
00:04:19,084 --> 00:04:20,535
Pause the video and try it.

62
00:04:20,985 --> 00:04:26,793
h of seven is five minus seven, which is
negative two. And five minus x equals two

63
00:04:26,793 --> 00:04:28,281
gives x equals three.

64
00:04:29,400 --> 00:04:33,496
Here's a worked example using function
notation in both directions.

65
00:04:34,933 --> 00:04:41,692
Let f of x equal two x squared minus three
x plus one. Find f of negative two, find f

66
00:04:41,692 --> 00:04:48,053
of a plus one, and solve g of x equals
eleven when g of x is three x minus four.

67
00:04:49,200 --> 00:04:53,275
For part a, replace every x with negative
two, in parentheses.

68
00:04:54,400 --> 00:04:59,904
Two times four is eight, minus negative
six is plus six, plus one. That's fifteen.

69
00:05:01,033 --> 00:05:04,660
For part b, replace x with the whole
expression a plus one.

70
00:05:05,800 --> 00:05:10,618
Expand: a plus one squared is a squared
plus two a plus one, times two. Then

71
00:05:10,618 --> 00:05:12,520
distribute the negative three.

72
00:05:13,667 --> 00:05:16,035
Combine like terms: two a squared plus a.

73
00:05:17,167 --> 00:05:23,334
For part c, g of x equals eleven means
three x minus four equals eleven. So three

74
00:05:23,334 --> 00:05:25,465
x is fifteen, and x is five.

75
00:05:26,600 --> 00:05:32,591
So f of negative two is fifteen, f of a
plus one is two a squared plus a, and g of

76
00:05:32,591 --> 00:05:34,856
x equals eleven when x is five.

77
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:41,639
Watch out. f of x does not mean f times x.
When you substitute an expression, keep it

78
00:05:41,639 --> 00:05:47,213
in parentheses. And notice the difference:
find f of three means plug in, but f of x

79
00:05:47,213 --> 00:05:49,269
equals three means solve for x.

80
00:05:49,400 --> 00:05:56,297
Your turn. For h of x equals x squared
minus four x, find h of negative three and

81
00:05:56,297 --> 00:05:59,573
h of t minus two. Pause the video and try
it.

82
00:06:04,100 --> 00:06:09,961
h of negative three is nine plus twelve,
which is twenty one. h of t minus two is t

83
00:06:09,961 --> 00:06:15,680
squared minus four t plus four, minus four
t plus eight, which is t squared minus

84
00:06:15,680 --> 00:06:17,092
eight t plus twelve.

85
00:06:18,233 --> 00:06:23,158
Every function has a domain and a range.
In this lesson, we'll learn what they are

86
00:06:23,158 --> 00:06:24,420
and how to find them.

87
00:06:25,566 --> 00:06:28,638
The domain is the set of all inputs: the x
values.

88
00:06:29,088 --> 00:06:32,224
The range is the set of all outputs: the y
values.

89
00:06:33,366 --> 00:06:37,526
Take the pairs one comma five, two comma
seven, and three comma five.

90
00:06:37,976 --> 00:06:42,956
The domain is one, two and three. The
range is five and seven. We list five only

91
00:06:42,956 --> 00:06:43,267
once.

92
00:06:44,400 --> 00:06:48,911
On a graph, the domain is how far the
graph stretches from left to right, and

93
00:06:48,911 --> 00:06:52,016
the range is how far it stretches from
bottom to top.

94
00:06:52,466 --> 00:06:58,055
This segment runs from x equals negative
two to x equals three. That's the domain.

95
00:06:58,505 --> 00:07:03,476
It goes from y equals negative one up to y
equals four. That's the range.

96
00:07:04,600 --> 00:07:10,958
Some formulas forbid certain inputs. For f
of x equals one over x minus two, x can't

97
00:07:10,958 --> 00:07:13,986
be two, because we can't divide by zero.

98
00:07:14,436 --> 00:07:17,167
So the domain is all real numbers except
two.

99
00:07:18,300 --> 00:07:24,290
In real situations, the context limits the
domain. A taxi charges three dollars plus

100
00:07:24,290 --> 00:07:25,788
two dollars per mile.

101
00:07:26,238 --> 00:07:31,325
The number of miles can't be negative, so
the domain is x greater than or equal to

102
00:07:31,325 --> 00:07:31,635
zero.

103
00:07:32,766 --> 00:07:37,758
Your turn. Find the domain and range of
the pairs negative one comma two, zero

104
00:07:37,758 --> 00:07:39,870
comma two, and three comma eight.

105
00:07:40,320 --> 00:07:41,771
Pause the video and try it.

106
00:07:42,221 --> 00:07:46,594
The domain is negative one, zero and
three. The range is two and eight.

107
00:07:47,733 --> 00:07:52,705
Let's find a domain and range for a real
situation, where the context sets the

108
00:07:52,705 --> 00:07:53,152
limits.

109
00:07:54,600 --> 00:07:59,771
A taxi charges three dollars plus two
dollars per mile, and trips are at most

110
00:07:59,771 --> 00:08:05,077
twenty miles. Write the cost function C of
m, then give a reasonable domain and

111
00:08:05,077 --> 00:08:05,480
range.

112
00:08:06,600 --> 00:08:12,197
The cost is two dollars per mile plus a
three dollar base, so C of m equals two m

113
00:08:12,197 --> 00:08:12,957
plus three.

114
00:08:14,100 --> 00:08:19,349
For the domain, miles can't be negative,
and trips are at most twenty miles. So m

115
00:08:19,349 --> 00:08:20,969
goes from zero to twenty.

116
00:08:22,100 --> 00:08:25,108
The smallest cost is at zero miles: three
dollars.

117
00:08:26,233 --> 00:08:29,689
The largest cost is at twenty miles: forty
three dollars.

118
00:08:30,833 --> 00:08:33,329
So the range is three to forty three
dollars.

119
00:08:34,466 --> 00:08:39,458
The domain is zero to twenty miles, and
the range is three to forty three dollars.

120
00:08:40,600 --> 00:08:45,358
Watch out. The domain is the inputs and
the range is the outputs. In a real

121
00:08:45,358 --> 00:08:50,686
situation, the context limits the domain.
And for a linear function, the range comes

122
00:08:50,686 --> 00:08:52,653
from plugging in the endpoints.

123
00:08:53,800 --> 00:08:58,880
Your turn. A pool holds five hundred
gallons and drains twenty five gallons a

124
00:08:58,880 --> 00:09:04,092
minute, so V of t is five hundred minus
twenty five t. Give a reasonable domain

125
00:09:04,092 --> 00:09:06,600
and range. Pause the video and try it.

126
00:09:11,133 --> 00:09:16,501
It's empty after twenty minutes, so the
domain is zero to twenty. The volume goes

127
00:09:16,501 --> 00:09:21,074
from five hundred down to zero, so the
range is zero to five hundred.

128
00:09:22,200 --> 00:09:26,701
Graphs tell stories. In this lesson, we'll
read information from the graph of a

129
00:09:26,701 --> 00:09:27,213
function.

130
00:09:28,600 --> 00:09:33,612
To find f of two from a graph, go to x
equals two, then move up or down to the

131
00:09:33,612 --> 00:09:33,997
curve.

132
00:09:34,447 --> 00:09:37,925
Here we reach y equals three, so f of two
equals three.

133
00:09:39,067 --> 00:09:43,867
To solve f of x equals zero, look for
where the graph meets the x axis.

134
00:09:44,317 --> 00:09:48,541
Here there are two points: x equals
negative one, and x equals three.

135
00:09:49,667 --> 00:09:54,895
Those points are the x intercepts. The
point where the graph crosses the y axis

136
00:09:54,895 --> 00:09:56,152
is the y intercept.

137
00:09:56,602 --> 00:10:00,229
Here, f of zero equals three, so the y
intercept is three.

138
00:10:01,367 --> 00:10:06,205
Reading from left to right, the graph goes
up until x equals one, so it's increasing

139
00:10:06,205 --> 00:10:06,551
there.

140
00:10:07,001 --> 00:10:12,469
After x equals one, it goes down, so it's
decreasing. The highest point, one comma

141
00:10:12,469 --> 00:10:13,870
four, is the maximum.

142
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:20,318
Graphs of real situations tell a story.
Suppose this graph shows the height of a

143
00:10:20,318 --> 00:10:21,315
ball over time.

144
00:10:21,765 --> 00:10:26,757
The ball rises, reaches its highest point
after one second, then falls back to the

145
00:10:26,757 --> 00:10:27,183
ground.

146
00:10:28,333 --> 00:10:33,646
Your turn. Using the same graph, find f of
three, and find every x where f of x

147
00:10:33,646 --> 00:10:34,520
equals three.

148
00:10:34,970 --> 00:10:36,421
Pause the video and try it.

149
00:10:36,871 --> 00:10:42,823
f of three is zero. And f of x equals
three at x equals zero and x equals two.

150
00:10:43,966 --> 00:10:47,294
Here's a worked example of reading key
features from a graph.

151
00:10:49,066 --> 00:10:54,902
Use the graph of f to find f of one, the x
values where f of x is zero, where f is

152
00:10:54,902 --> 00:10:57,322
increasing, and its maximum value.

153
00:10:58,466 --> 00:11:03,095
For f of one, go to x equals one and read
the height of the graph. It's three.

154
00:11:04,233 --> 00:11:10,535
f of x equals zero where the graph crosses
the x axis: at x equals zero and x equals

155
00:11:10,535 --> 00:11:10,910
four.

156
00:11:12,033 --> 00:11:17,559
Reading left to right, the graph goes up
until x equals two. So f is increasing for

157
00:11:17,559 --> 00:11:18,625
x less than two.

158
00:11:19,766 --> 00:11:24,512
The highest point is at x equals two,
where the height is four. The maximum

159
00:11:24,512 --> 00:11:25,398
value is four.

160
00:11:26,533 --> 00:11:32,581
So f of one is three, the zeros are zero
and four, f increases for x less than two,

161
00:11:32,581 --> 00:11:34,767
and the maximum value is four.

162
00:11:35,899 --> 00:11:40,858
Watch out. f of one is a height, a y
value. Increasing and decreasing are

163
00:11:40,858 --> 00:11:46,020
described with x intervals. And the
maximum value is the y coordinate of the

164
00:11:46,020 --> 00:11:48,465
highest point, not its x coordinate.

165
00:11:49,599 --> 00:11:54,496
Your turn. Using the same graph, where is
f decreasing, and what is f of three?

166
00:11:54,496 --> 00:11:56,170
Pause the video and try it.

167
00:12:00,699 --> 00:12:06,133
f is decreasing for x greater than two,
and f of three is three, by symmetry with

168
00:12:06,133 --> 00:12:06,737
f of one.

169
00:12:07,866 --> 00:12:12,762
A sequence is an ordered list of numbers.
In this lesson, we'll study arithmetic

170
00:12:12,762 --> 00:12:16,250
sequences, and see that they are really
linear functions.

171
00:12:17,399 --> 00:12:22,050
In five, eight, eleven, fourteen, each
term is three more than the one before.

172
00:12:22,500 --> 00:12:25,700
That constant difference, three, is called
the common difference.

173
00:12:26,833 --> 00:12:32,209
The nth term is the first term, plus n
minus one times the common difference.

174
00:12:32,659 --> 00:12:36,371
Here, a sub n equals five plus three times
n minus one.

175
00:12:37,499 --> 00:12:40,614
To find the twentieth term, plug in n
equals twenty.

176
00:12:41,064 --> 00:12:45,907
Five plus three times nineteen is five
plus fifty-seven, which is sixty-two.

177
00:12:47,033 --> 00:12:52,025
Simplify the formula: five plus three n
minus three is three n plus two.

178
00:12:52,475 --> 00:12:57,544
Plot the terms as points: one comma five,
two comma eight, three comma eleven. They

179
00:12:57,544 --> 00:13:01,392
lie on a straight line with slope three,
the common difference.

180
00:13:02,533 --> 00:13:07,054
The common difference can be negative. In
twenty, sixteen, twelve, eight, the

181
00:13:07,054 --> 00:13:08,698
difference is negative four.

182
00:13:09,148 --> 00:13:13,866
The formula is twenty minus four times n
minus one, which simplifies to twenty-four

183
00:13:13,866 --> 00:13:15,761
minus four n.

184
00:13:16,899 --> 00:13:21,259
Your turn. For the sequence two, nine,
sixteen, twenty-three, find the common

185
00:13:21,259 --> 00:13:22,958
difference and the tenth term.

186
00:13:23,408 --> 00:13:24,859
Pause the video and try it.

187
00:13:25,309 --> 00:13:30,374
The common difference is seven. The tenth
term is two plus seven times nine, which

188
00:13:30,374 --> 00:13:31,239
is sixty-five.

189
00:13:32,366 --> 00:13:36,974
Let's find a formula for an arithmetic
sequence when you only know two terms.

190
00:13:38,399 --> 00:13:43,648
In an arithmetic sequence, the fourth term
is seventeen and the tenth term is forty

191
00:13:43,648 --> 00:13:48,771
one. Find the common difference, a formula
for the nth term, and the twenty fifth

192
00:13:48,771 --> 00:13:49,087
term.

193
00:13:50,233 --> 00:13:55,425
From term four to term ten there are six
steps, each adding d. So the difference is

194
00:13:55,425 --> 00:13:55,801
six d.

195
00:13:56,933 --> 00:14:02,310
Forty one minus seventeen is twenty four,
so six d equals twenty four, and d is

196
00:14:02,310 --> 00:14:02,650
four.

197
00:14:03,799 --> 00:14:08,861
To get the first term, step back three
terms from a four: seventeen minus twelve

198
00:14:08,861 --> 00:14:09,367
is five.

199
00:14:10,499 --> 00:14:16,080
The formula is a one plus n minus one
times d, which simplifies to four n plus

200
00:14:16,080 --> 00:14:16,366
one.

201
00:14:17,499 --> 00:14:22,513
The twenty fifth term is four times twenty
five plus one, which is one hundred one.

202
00:14:23,633 --> 00:14:28,680
The common difference is four, the formula
is four n plus one, and the twenty fifth

203
00:14:28,680 --> 00:14:30,139
term is one hundred one.

204
00:14:31,266 --> 00:14:36,065
Watch out. Count the steps between the
terms, which is ten minus four, or six.

205
00:14:36,065 --> 00:14:40,618
Use n minus one in the formula. And
remember, the common difference is the

206
00:14:40,618 --> 00:14:42,402
slope of the linear function.

207
00:14:43,533 --> 00:14:48,721
Your turn. The third term is two and the
eighth term is negative eighteen. Find d,

208
00:14:48,721 --> 00:14:52,770
the formula, and the twentieth term. Pause
the video and try it.

209
00:14:57,299 --> 00:15:02,656
Five steps change the value by negative
twenty, so d is negative four. a one is

210
00:15:02,656 --> 00:15:08,013
two plus eight, or ten. The formula is
fourteen minus four n, and the twentieth

211
00:15:08,013 --> 00:15:09,843
term is negative sixty six.

212
00:15:10,966 --> 00:15:16,020
Let's finish Unit Four with one real
problem that uses functions, notation,

213
00:15:16,020 --> 00:15:18,177
domain and range, and sequences.

214
00:15:19,599 --> 00:15:24,135
Maya has thirty five dollars saved, and
she saves twelve dollars more each week

215
00:15:24,135 --> 00:15:28,729
until she can buy a two hundred fifteen
dollar bike. Write her savings function,

216
00:15:28,729 --> 00:15:33,380
find when she can buy the bike, give the
domain and range, and decide whether the

217
00:15:33,380 --> 00:15:35,791
weekly totals form an arithmetic sequence.

218
00:15:36,933 --> 00:15:42,757
Her savings start at thirty five and grow
by twelve per week, so S of w equals

219
00:15:42,757 --> 00:15:44,698
thirty five plus twelve w.

220
00:15:45,833 --> 00:15:49,886
She can buy the bike when S of w equals
two hundred fifteen.

221
00:15:51,033 --> 00:15:56,473
Subtract thirty five: twelve w is one
hundred eighty, so w is fifteen weeks.

222
00:15:57,599 --> 00:16:00,522
The domain is the whole weeks from zero to
fifteen.

223
00:16:01,666 --> 00:16:06,865
The range is the matching savings: thirty
five, forty seven, fifty nine, and so on

224
00:16:06,865 --> 00:16:08,514
up to two hundred fifteen.

225
00:16:09,633 --> 00:16:13,891
The totals go up by the same twelve
dollars each week, so yes, it's an

226
00:16:13,891 --> 00:16:16,993
arithmetic sequence, with common
difference twelve.

227
00:16:18,133 --> 00:16:23,469
Maya's savings are thirty five plus twelve
w, and she can buy the bike after fifteen

228
00:16:23,469 --> 00:16:23,850
weeks.

229
00:16:24,999 --> 00:16:29,699
Watch out. The starting amount is the
output when w is zero. Because weeks are

230
00:16:29,699 --> 00:16:34,640
whole numbers, the graph is separate dots,
not a solid line. And a constant change

231
00:16:34,640 --> 00:16:39,399
means the function is linear, and the list
of values is an arithmetic sequence.

232
00:16:40,533 --> 00:16:45,621
Your turn. A plant is eight centimeters
tall and grows one and a half centimeters

233
00:16:45,621 --> 00:16:50,771
per week. Write H of w and find when it
reaches twenty nine centimeters. Pause the

234
00:16:50,771 --> 00:16:51,839
video and try it.

235
00:16:56,366 --> 00:17:03,182
H of w equals eight plus one point five w.
Set it equal to twenty nine: one point

236
00:17:03,182 --> 00:17:06,969
five w is twenty one, so w is fourteen
weeks.

